        "problem": "Simplify $\\frac{3}{a(b-3)}+\\frac{1}{a}.$",
        "worked_solution": "In order to add the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. Here, we can put both fractions over a common denominator of $a(b-3)$ if we multiply the top and bottom of the second fraction by $b-3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{a(b-3)}+\\frac{1}{a} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{a(b-3)}+\\frac{b-3}{b-3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{a} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{a(b-3)}+\\frac{b-3}{a(b-3)} & = \\\\ \\frac{3+b-3}{a(b-3)} & = \\\\ \\frac{b}{a(b-3)} & \\end{aligned}\n$$",
